          :distillation
          {:enabled? false :provider "ollama" :model nil
           :batch-size 20 :max-capsules 20}}
 ;; Background worker runs. Concurrency is deliberately small: these share the
 ;; local model with interactive chat.
 :worker {:max-concurrency 2
          :max-runs 50}

 ;; The stdio MCP server and separately authenticated HTTP transport. Fleet
 ;; tools need no session over stdio; funding and settlement
 ;; tools need a real one, and appear in the manifest only when this token
 ;; resolves to a live session whose user has enrolled a Passkey. Absent token =
 ;; no such tools, which is the shipped default.
 ;;
 ;; The token is an app session token. It is read from this environment variable,
 ;; or from the login Keychain (service `cloud-itonami-app.mcp`, account
 ;; `session-token`) — never stored here.
 ;; Two slots, because the two surfaces need sessions that are required to
 ;; differ: the agent one is minted by `auth login`, the human one has to belong
 ;; to a Passkey-enrolled user (ADR-0009). Sharing one meant enrolling the CLI
 ;; silently disabled funding and settlement.
 ;; HTTP is disabled until both enabled? and a token in access-token-env are
 ;; present. Its actor-user-id scopes Workspace and AgentRun tools.
 :mcp {:session-token-env "CLOUD_ITONAMI_MCP_SESSION"
       :human-session-token-env "CLOUD_ITONAMI_MCP_HUMAN_SESSION"
       :enabled? false
       :access-token-env "CLOUD_ITONAMI_MCP_ACCESS_TOKEN"
       :actor-user-id nil
       :clients []}

 ;; 事業 (ADR-2607309600). A Business holds one key from each analysis plane —
 ;; BMC canvas, XMILE model, adopted blueprints, repo paths, LEI — so that the
 ;; three planes that cannot currently join to each other meet on one entity.
 ;;
 ;; :workspace-root is a checkout of the com-junkawasaki/root superproject, which
 ;; is where those planes actually live (90-docs/, manifest/). nil is the shipped
 ;; default because this app is released on its own and cannot assume a west
 ;; checkout beside it — the same reason `fleet` ships its catalog as a resource.
 ;; Unset does NOT mean 'nothing found': every plane-backed face reports
 ;; :unresolvable and names this setting, because a face that read 'missing' when
 ;; nobody said where to look would be a measurement of nothing.
 :business {:workspace-root nil
            ;; How old a metrics/<product>.edn may be and still read as current.
            ;; 3 days: the emitter runs daily, so one missed run is not staleness
            ;; and three are. Measured 2026-07-30: eleven of twelve metrics files
            ;; were same-day, and ai-gftd-yukkuri was 28 days old — which is the
            ;; case this exists to mark rather than print as a current figure.
            :metrics-max-age-days 3}

 ;; Verifiable Credentials issued by OTHER organizations
 ;; (cloud.itonami.app.credential-trust). Credentials this app issued itself need
 ;; nothing here — they are checked against its own key.
 ;;
 ;; :trusted-issuers is DENY-BY-DEFAULT and there is no way to switch it off,
 ;; because for did:web the trust list is not hardening, it is the trust model.
 ;; Anyone can publish a DID document at a domain they control and sign a
 ;; credential with the matching key; verifying that proves only "whoever
 ;; controls that domain said this". A verifier that fetches the key the
 ;; credential names is checking the forger's arithmetic against the forger's own
 ;; key. So the operator decides which domains to believe, in advance, here.
 ;;
 ;; nil accepts nothing. [] accepts nothing and says so deliberately -- an absent
 ;; policy is not a permissive policy, the same reading :authorities :voice
 ;; :allowed-callers already documents.
 :credentials {:trusted-issuers nil
               :fetch-timeout-seconds 5
               ;; A DID document is a handful of keys; the cap is what stops a
               ;; trusted-but-compromised domain streaming until this process
               ;; runs out of heap.
               :max-document-bytes 65536
               ;; Cached per verificationMethod rather than per domain, so a key
               ;; rotation is not answered from a stale entry for a sibling key.
               :cache-seconds 3600
               ;; Status lists cache for far less time than keys: a key rotates
               ;; rarely, a revocation is the thing being asked about. The list's
               ;; own `ttl` wins when it is shorter, because the issuer knows how
               ;; often it republishes.
               ;;
               ;; Revocation is checked, and FAILS CLOSED: a list that cannot be
               ;; fetched, does not verify, or is signed by someone other than the
               ;; credential's own issuer comes back :unchecked and the credential
               ;; is not treated as valid. "Could not ask" is not "not revoked".
               :status-cache-seconds 300}

 ;; Governed outward authorities (ADR-2607300300). Each rides the
 ;; `cloud.itonami.app.authority` spine: deterministic pre-check -> Passkey
 ;; consent -> hand-off to an actor that has its own Governor and ledger.
 ;;
 ;; ALL DISABLED BY DEFAULT, the same posture as :agent-control. These are the
 ;; only surfaces in this app that carry a proposal outward to something that
 ;; could affect a real card, a real line or a real phone call, so switching one
 ;; on is a deployment decision and not a default.
 ;;
 ;; `:endpoint` is a BASE url (e.g. "http://127.0.0.1:1339"), from which the
 ;; transport derives `/commit` and `/proposals/<reference>`. nil means there is
 ;; nowhere to hand a proposal to, so an enabled authority with no endpoint still
 ;; cannot commit.
 ;;
 ;; Only the actor's CONSENT surface belongs here. The operator surface -- where a
 ;; pending proposal is actually decided -- is a different listener on purpose, and
 ;; this app must not be given its address: holding both would mean holding both
 ;; gates.
 ;;
 ;; The consent surfaces, as the actors ship them (all loopback, `clojure -M:serve`):
 ;;
 ;;   esim   http://127.0.0.1:1339   cloud-itonami-esim          (operator :1340)
 ;;   card   http://127.0.0.1:1341   cloud-itonami-card-issuing  (operator :1342)
 ;;   voice  http://127.0.0.1:1343   denwaban                    (no operator port)
 ;;
 ;; denwaban has no operator port on purpose: its outward gate G7 is shut, so an
 ;; approval there could not answer a call, and every op comes back `held`.
 ;;
 ;; CARD IS NOW THE EXCEPTION to "propose-only". The other two actors cannot perform
 ;; an outward act at all, so a :committed proposal there records a governed proposal
 ;; and NOT a downloaded profile or an answered call -- a UI that reads it as "done"
 ;; is lying. cloud-itonami-card-issuing CAN issue a real card, through Stripe
 ;; Issuing, when its operator approves AND its deployment injects the provider
 ;; (CARD_ACTUATOR=1; CARD_ACTUATOR_MODE=live for real money, test mode otherwise).
 ;; That is why the transport now understands a fourth answer,
 ;; "approved-not-actuated": the operator's approval and the provider's call are
 ;; separate events that fail separately, and collapsing them would either lose an
 ;; approval that happened or claim a card exists when Stripe declined.
 ;;
 ;; Nothing in THIS app holds a Stripe key. The provider reads it from an
 ;; environment variable named by its own caller, at call time, inside the actor's
 ;; process.
 ;; :consent-token-env names the ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE holding the shared secret this
 ;; app presents to an actor's consent surface -- never the value. A token written here
 ;; would be a secret in git, and this particular one is what lets a caller claim a
 ;; subject consented. The actor refuses (503) when its own side is unconfigured, which
 ;; is the honest failure; this app does not pre-emptively refuse on the actor's behalf.
 :authorities
 {:esim {:enabled? false
         :endpoint nil    ; cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-esim
         :consent-token-env "ESIM_CONSENT_TOKEN"}
  :card {:enabled? false
         :endpoint nil    ; cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-card-issuing
         :consent-token-env "CARD_CONSENT_TOKEN"   ; actor: X-CARD-CONSENT-TOKEN
         ;; Pre-check arithmetic only. The issuer's own limits are the issuer's;
         ;; this is the app refusing to ask a human about something it can already
         ;; tell will exceed the funding account's recorded daily limit.
         :default-daily-limit nil
         ;; The cross-domain invariant (ADR-2607300300 D4): how long an eSIM
         ;; ownership transfer keeps this subject's card spend and issuance
         ;; restricted. Seven days by default -- long enough to cover the
         ;; days-scale tail of a real account takeover, short enough that a
         ;; legitimate device change does not restrict a subject indefinitely.
         ;; This is only expressible because all three authorities' proposals
         ;; share one partition; see cloud.itonami.app.authority.posture.
         :sim-swap-window-seconds 604800}
  ;; Settlement records for money the organization owes. The deterministic gate
  ;; is the funds check: a payment whose recorded balance does not cover it is
  ;; refused BEFORE a human is asked. A committed proposal is a governed
  ;; settlement RECORD -- this app holds no banking credential and moves no
  ;; money; a human makes the transfer in their bank.
  :payment {:enabled? false
            :endpoint nil  ; cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-settlement
            ;; What kind of authenticator may approve a settlement.
            ;;
            ;; The decision (2026-07-30) was a Secure Enclave key requiring
            ;; Touch ID, so that an agent cannot approve on the owner's behalf.
            ;; A Passkey assertion alone does not establish that: a browser's
            ;; virtual authenticator verifies identically and signs with no
            ;; human present. cloud.itonami.app.credential-assurance grades the
            ;; difference from evidence captured at enrolment.
            ;;
            ;; :platform-claimed is the shipped floor because it is the
            ;; strongest one KNOWN to be reachable. :platform-attested needs the
            ;; browser to disclose a real AAGUID under direct attestation, and
            ;; whether it does is measurable on the machine rather than
            ;; guessable here. Register once, read
            ;; GET /api/passkeys/assurance, then raise this with evidence --
            ;; a floor the hardware cannot clear would enrol fine and then
            ;; refuse every payment, which reads as a bug.
            :credential-policy {:min-assurance :platform-claimed
                                :require-user-verification? true}
            ;; How old a recorded balance may be and still answer 'will this
            ;; clear?'. 24h: a company current account moves on business-day
            ;; timescales. Beyond this the pre-check refuses with
            ;; :payment/balance-unknown rather than trusting a stale figure --
            ;; an absent balance is not an unlimited one.
            :balance-max-age-seconds 86400}
  :voice {:enabled? false
          :endpoint nil   ; cloud-itonami/denwaban (R0: run-session raises at G7)
          :consent-token-env "VOICE_CONSENT_TOKEN"
          ;; nil = no allowlist configured, so the pre-check does not apply one.
          ;; An EMPTY vector means nothing is allowed -- an absent policy is not a
          ;; permissive policy, and `[]` is how you say "no callers yet".
          :allowed-callers nil}}}
